UNIT explain_contract
KIND FUNCTIONAL
FIDELITY package
ANCHOR rederive@9b9e5d24ca9d cli/lsp/explain.mjs explain(primaryText) called with no options, over the parse of
  cli/sir/sir-parse.mjs in its default (legacy) mode; reproduce both exactly, accidents included
ORACLE-CLASS deterministic
SIG explain_contract(primaryText: text) -> json
ENVELOPE primaryText is any well-formed text (Unicode scalar values only), empty included: a SIR primary, a language
  file, a legacy v0.1 contract with comments, or text that is not a contract at all. Every such text yields a reading;
  none throws. Non-text arguments are outside the abstract envelope (the js language file widens to them).
DEPENDS-ON none
DESCRIPTION The deterministic plain-English reading of one SIR primary, the one a review renders. The result is a map
  with five entries: unit (text), summary (a list of text), sig (text or none), checks (a list of text, always empty
  here) and sections (a list). Each section is a map: id (text), title (text), checked (bool) and items (a non-empty
  list). Each item is a map: id (text), label (text, absent for the directives TABLE SECTIONS gives no label), text
  (text), why (a list of text), raw (text), rawWhy (a list of text) and line (a whole number). text and why are the
  reading; raw and rawWhy are what an edit is compared on; line is where the directive (or stray line) starts. The
  reading never rewrites contract text: every text is the contract's own words verbatim, or a fixed phrase from the
  rules and tables below.
BEHAVIOR
R1 Lines. The text is cut into lines at every carriage return followed by a line feed (one break), every other
  carriage return, and every line feed; no other character breaks a line (LINE SEPARATOR, PARAGRAPH SEPARATOR, NEXT
  LINE, form feed and vertical tab are ordinary characters). Lines are numbered from 1 and every line counts,
  blank ones included; the empty text is one empty line. On each line, every U+FEFF inside its leading run of
  SPACE-TAB characters and U+FEFF marks is deleted (the spaces and tabs of that run stay), then the trailing run of
  SPACE-TAB characters is deleted. A line that is then empty is BLANK and is skipped.
R2 INDENT of a line = its leading run of SPACE-TAB characters (possibly empty); CONTENT = the rest (never empty, never
  starting with a space or tab). A line whose CONTENT starts with "#" is a COMMENT line, at any indent. When a
  directive has been started (R5), the comment line does not end it: its COMMENT TEXT (CONTENT without its leading run
  of "#" characters, and then without ONE space or tab if one follows) is appended to the COMMENTS of the most
  recently started directive, whatever directive the comment seems to introduce (a comment written just above R2
  belongs to the directive before R2). Before the first directive, a comment line is a STRAY line (R17)
  CITED FIDELITY package — architect's ruling N3: parser semantics, kept
R3 QUOTED SPANS of a string, found left to right: a quotation mark (U+0022) OPENS a span when it is the first
  character, or the character before it is in OPENERS, or it comes directly after the closing mark of the previous
  span. The span runs to the next quotation mark not escaped: a backslash escapes the ONE code point after it. An
  opener with no closing mark ends the search (no span from it, and none after it). A quotation mark that does not
  open (after a letter, a digit, "/", "*", "+", "?", a closing quote of no span, ...) is an ordinary character. A
  position is INSIDE a span when it lies strictly between the span's opening and closing marks. R4 and R6 use the
  spans of the line's CONTENT; R13 uses the spans of a pin's ARGS.
R4 Trailing comment. The MARKER is the first "#" of CONTENT that is preceded by a space or tab, is followed by a space,
  a tab or the end of CONTENT, and is not INSIDE a quoted span (R3). CODE = CONTENT up to the MARKER with its
  trailing SPACE-TAB run deleted, or all of CONTENT when there is no MARKER. The TRAILING COMMENT = CONTENT after the
  MARKER with its leading and trailing SPACE-TAB runs deleted (possibly empty). On a line that starts or continues a
  directive (R5, R7) it is appended to that directive's COMMENTS; it is never part of the display text. So "a #b" and
  "a##" keep their "#"; "a # b" is CODE "a" with the comment "b"; "a #" is CODE "a" with an empty comment. Comment text is lossy by
  design: "# x" and "## x" give the same COMMENT TEXT (R2), and moving a comment between a trailing and a full line
  leaves RAW unchanged CITED FIDELITY package — the legacy parse keeps v0.1 comments aside; architect's ruling N9: kept
R5 Directive lines. A line with an empty INDENT whose CODE is NAME followed by REST: NAME = CODE up to its first space
  or tab (all of CODE when it has none), REST = the remainder from that space or tab on (empty when none). When NAME
  is one capital ASCII letter, then capital letters, ASCII digits and hyphens, then a final colon, and NAME without
  the colon is not CITED, the colon is dropped (the legacy "UNIT: x" spelling). The line STARTS A DIRECTIVE when NAME
  matches the grammar DIRECTIVE-NAME and is not CITED. The directive's name is NAME, its line number the line's, its
  ARGS and ATTRIBUTES come from REST by R6, its CONT starts empty, its COMMENTS start with the line's trailing comment
  (if any), and it is KNOWN when NAME is in VOCABULARY or is "R" followed by digits. Every other line with an empty
  INDENT (a lowercase start, "CITED x", "UNIT:glued", a name glued to its text by a character other than a space or
  tab such as U+00A0, a leading U+00A0 or U+2060) is a continuation by R7.
R6 Attribute split of a piece S (REST of a directive line, or a space followed by CODE for a continuation). An
  ATTRIBUTE KEYWORD is one of CITED, EARNED, ANCHOR, case-sensitive, that is preceded by at least one space or tab,
  followed by a space, a tab or the end of S, and whose first letter is not INSIDE a quoted span of the line (R3).
  BODY = S before the space-or-tab run that precedes the first keyword (all of S when there is none). Each keyword
  yields the pair (keyword, value), value = S from just after the keyword up to the space-or-tab run before the
  next keyword (or the end); BODY and every value have their leading and trailing SPACE-TAB runs deleted, and a
  value may be empty. Pairs keep their order; a keyword may repeat. A word that only contains a keyword
  ("aCITED", "CITEDx", "CITED:") is not one.
R7 Continuations. An indented line that is not a COMMENT line, or a column-0 line that does not start a directive
  (R5), belongs to the most recently started directive: S = a space followed by its CODE is split by R6, the pairs
  are appended to that directive's ATTRIBUTES, BODY, when not empty, is appended to its CONT, and then the line's
  trailing comment (if any) to its COMMENTS. Such a line before the first directive is a STRAY line (R17).
R8 TEXT of a directive = ARGS followed by each CONT piece, the empty ones left out, joined by single spaces; for a
  directive named TABLE the joiner is a space, U+23CE (RETURN SYMBOL), a space. TEXT is the empty text when all pieces are
  empty. RAW of a directive = the same pieces joined by single spaces, except that for TABLE the joiner is
  a line feed (U+000A), so a literal U+23CE inside a row never reads as a row boundary in RAW; then, for each of its
  COMMENTS in order, " # " and that comment. In the display a literal U+23CE inside a TABLE row looks like a row
  boundary; RAW tells the two apart CITED FIDELITY package — architect's ruling N4: kept
R9 Result. HEAD(n, legacy) = the first directive with a non-empty TEXT among every directive named n (in contract
  order) followed by every directive named legacy (in contract order); when none has text, the first named n, else
  the first named legacy, else none. unit = TEXT of HEAD(UNIT, SIR), or "(unnamed)" when there is none or that TEXT
  is empty. summary, in order: when HEAD(KIND, KIND) has a non-empty TEXT T, the KIND-PHRASE row of T, or "Kind: " T
  " (not a kind SIR defines)." when T has no row; when HEAD(FIDELITY, VARIANT) has a non-empty TEXT T, the
  FIDELITY-PHRASE row of T, or "Fidelity: " T " (not a fidelity SIR defines)." when T has no row; then, for the first ANCHOR directive (in
  contract order) whose TEXT is still non-empty once its trailing run of "." is deleted, "Anchored to " that shortened
  TEXT ".". No KIND with text, no FIDELITY or VARIANT with text, or no such ANCHOR -> that sentence is left out. Only
  the trailing run counts: an ANCHOR whose TEXT is ". .." shortens to ". " and gives "Anchored to . .". sig = TEXT of
  the first SIG directive (not HEAD), or none when there is no SIG. checks = the empty list.
R10 Sections, in the order of TABLE SECTIONS; a section with no item is left out. Within a section, the items come
  member by member in the listed order, and for each member every directive of that name in contract order. The
  rules section holds every directive named "R" followed by digits, in contract order (R1 and R01 are two distinct
  rules, ids R1 and R01, labels "Rule 1" and "Rule 01"). A blank line makes no item.
R11 Item of a directive d with label L and shown text X (X = TEXT of d unless a rule below says otherwise): id by
  R12; label L when the table gives one; text = X, verbatim; why = for each attribute pair of d in order, by TABLE
  WHY; raw = RAW of d; rawWhy = for each pair, keyword " " value, or the keyword alone when the value is empty;
  line = d's line.
R12 Item ids. The BASE of an item is its directive's name; for PINNED it is "PIN:" followed by the pin NAME (R13), or
  "PIN" when NAME is empty; for a stray item it is "STRAY". Counting runs over the items in the order the sections
  are listed (so a directive named STRAY, shown in "more", is counted before every stray item). The first item of a
  BASE has id BASE, the k-th (k >= 2) has id BASE " #" k CITED FIDELITY package — architect's ruling N7: kept
R13 PINNED parts. EQ = the first "=" of ARGS that is not INSIDE a quoted span of ARGS (R3); HEAD = ARGS up to EQ (all
  of ARGS when there is no EQ), with its leading and trailing SPACE-TAB runs deleted, cut into words at runs of space
  and tab; kind = the first word, NAME = the second (each empty when missing; further words are ignored). When there
  is an EQ: VALUE = the part of ARGS after EQ with its leading and trailing SPACE-TAB runs deleted (any other
  whitespace, U+00A0 included, is kept), followed by the CONT pieces, the empty ones left out, joined by single
  spaces; the item's shown text is VALUE (the empty text when VALUE is empty). When there is no EQ, the shown text
  is TEXT of the pin (kind and name included). Its label is NAME, or "(unnamed pin)" when empty, then " (", then the
  PIN-KIND row of kind, else kind itself, else "no kind" when kind is empty, then ", empty value" when there is an EQ
  and VALUE is empty, then ")". So continuation lines appear in two shapes: after the VALUE of a pin with an EQ, or
  after the kind and name of a pin without one CITED FIDELITY package — architect's ruling N5: kept
R14 DEPENDS-ON. When TEXT of the directive (comments never count) is "none" in any mix of ASCII upper and lower case,
  the item's text is "Nothing. No other package is involved." (why, raw and rawWhy unchanged); otherwise the general
  rule holds.
R15 Other parts. The "more" section holds, in contract order, every directive whose name is not a member of an earlier
  or later section in TABLE SECTIONS and is not "R" followed by digits, except a BEHAVIOR whose TEXT is empty and
  that has no attribute pair. Its label: name " (not in the SIR vocabulary)" when the directive is not KNOWN (R5),
  else the OTHER-LABEL row of the name, else the name itself. Language-file and legacy names (LANG, ABI, SIR,
  VARIANT, NOTES, ...) are KNOWN and so are labelled by their bare name. The section is checked whatever it holds
  (an unknown or prose-only name included) CITED FIDELITY package — architect's ruling F6: it errs toward flagging
R16 CARRIED. The label is the first word of ARGS (up to its first space or tab), or "Carried data" when ARGS is empty.
R17 Stray lines. The "stray" section holds one item per STRAY line (R2, R7), in line order: id by R12; label "Line "
  followed by the line number; text = raw = the line after R1 with its leading SPACE-TAB run deleted (so a trailing
  comment on it stays in the text, unlike a directive's display text); why and rawWhy empty; line = the line number
  CITED FIDELITY package — architect's ruling N10: kept
TABLE SECTIONS — order | id | title | checked | members, each with its item label ("-" = no label)
  1  | header | What kind of unit it is      | true  | UNIT Name; KIND Kind; FIDELITY Fidelity; ANCHOR Anchor
  2  | what   | What it is for               | false | DESCRIPTION -
  3  | inputs | What it accepts              | true  | SIG Signature; ENVELOPE Inputs it is verified on; UNGUARDED-PRECONDITION Assumes, without checking; OUT-OF-DOMAIN Not covered
  4  | rules  | What it does                 | true  | R<digits> "Rule " followed by the name without its R (leading zeros kept)
  5  | pins   | Exact definitions            | true  | PINNED by R13
  6  | table  | Decision table               | true  | TABLE -
  7  | data   | Data it carries              | true  | CARRIED by R16
  8  | deps   | What it depends on           | true  | DEPENDS-ON - (text by R14)
  9  | more   | Other parts of the contract  | true  | everything else, by R15
  10 | stray  | Text outside any directive   | true  | the STRAY lines, by R17
  11 | holds  | Should always hold           | false | INVARIANT -
  12 | scope  | In and out of scope          | false | SCOPE -
  13 | notes  | Background                   | false | NOTE Note; RATIONALE Why; PROVENANCE Where it came from
TABLE WHY — attribute pair | the why text
  CITED v, v not empty   | v
  CITED, empty value     | no reason given
  EARNED v, v not empty  | added after a failed test (v)
  EARNED, empty value    | added after a failed test
  ANCHOR v, v not empty  | anchored to v
  ANCHOR, empty value    | anchored (no reference given)
TABLE KIND-PHRASE — TEXT of HEAD(KIND, KIND) (exact, case-sensitive) | summary sentence
  FUNCTIONAL | A pure function: the same input always gives the same output.
  STATE      | Changes one of its arguments in place; the tests check the result and the changed argument.
  EFFECT     | Talks to the outside world; the tests check the calls it makes, in order.
TABLE FIDELITY-PHRASE — TEXT of HEAD(FIDELITY, VARIANT) (exact, case-sensitive) | summary sentence
  package | Matches the original package exactly, quirks included.
  spec    | Follows the cited standard, even where the original package does not.
TABLE PIN-KIND — pin kind (exact, case-sensitive) | phrase in the label
  regex | pattern
  charset | character set
  grammar | grammar
  text | exact text
  set | set
  const | constant
  names | names
  number | number
  constant | constant
  chars | characters
  order | order
  map | mapping
  bytes | bytes
TABLE OTHER-LABEL — directive name | label in the "more" section
  ORACLE | The tests (file and fingerprint)
  ORACLE-CLASS | Kind of tests
  STATE | What it changes in place
  SEAM | Export shape
  TRACE-SEAM | Effect boundary
  SRC | Source location
  EFFECT | Effect
  SURFACE | Module surface
  EMIT | Emits
  REQUEST | Requests
  BRANCH | Branch
  ON-ERROR | On error
  PAR | Unordered window
  RING | Token ring
  LIFECYCLE | Resource lifecycle
  REACH | Off-path unit
  TRIGGER | Trigger
  EARNED | Earned rule
  BEHAVIOR | Behavior header
PINNED grammar DIRECTIVE-NAME = CAPITAL ( CAPITAL | DIGIT | "-" )* | "R" DIGIT+ over the WHOLE name, CAPITAL = A to Z, DIGIT = 0 to 9 (ASCII only) CITED R5 — sir-parse.mjs isDirectiveName
PINNED names VOCABULARY = UNIT SIR KIND FIDELITY VARIANT ANCHOR SRC TRIGGER REACH ORACLE-CLASS TRACE-SEAM SEAM SIG ENVELOPE DEPENDS-ON DESCRIPTION BEHAVIOR INVARIANT TABLE PINNED EARNED NOTE RATIONALE SCOPE UNGUARDED-PRECONDITION OUT-OF-DOMAIN ORACLE PROVENANCE CARRIED SHAPE CANONICAL-SHA256 AUTHORITY CARRIED-DATA STATE EMIT REQUEST BRANCH ON-ERROR PAR RING LIFECYCLE EFFECT SURFACE NOTES GUARD-PINNED LANG PRIMARY-SHA256 ABI MECHANISM (49 names, case-sensitive) CITED R5 R15 — the sir-parse.mjs directive table
PINNED charset SPACE-TAB = the code points 0009 0020 (hexadecimal: tab, space) CITED R1 R2 R4 R6 R13 — the parser's one whitespace rule, the only trim the reading uses
PINNED charset OPENERS = the code points 0009 0020 0028 005B 007B 002C 003D 003A 003B 007C 003C 003E 0021 0026 002D 007E (tab space ( [ { , = : ; | < > ! & - ~) CITED R3 R13 — sir-parse.mjs quotedSpans, round 5b
INVARIANT a section never has an empty item list, and every item id is unique within the reading.
INVARIANT text equals TEXT of its directive for every item except DEPENDS-ON none (R14) and pins (R13); raw differs
  from TEXT only by comments and the TABLE line feeds (R8).
OUT-OF-DOMAIN non-text input: the host fails reading its lines and throws (js widening; the message is not graded).
ORACLE value -> oracles/explain_contract.json  CANONICAL-SHA256 a0ce0171381f1cfb903f4778318ef5c35c18a811ef4af74fee55f42b06c35ba1
NOTE Rust is left out by domain: the review system ships as JavaScript (rdv review and the rederive/sir-review GitHub
  Action); there is no Rust consumer.
SCOPE IN: R1-R17 over ENVELOPE. OUT: the options (langs, oracle) and the checks they produce; explainDiff, wordDiff
  and oracleDiff in the same module; the parser's warnings, error messages and indentation statistics, which the
  reading never shows.
PROVENANCE rederive@9b9e5d24ca9d (rederive, MIT) cli/lsp/explain.mjs explain, cli/sir/sir-parse.mjs parse and
  sirLines, via the seam selfhost/seams/explain_contract.mjs. Cut cold 2026-11-02 for the selfhost phase 2 (the review
  system), from the pinned snapshot only; re-cut the same day on 9e2245be5f42 (rulings F1-F15),
  65149601a522 (rulings N1-N10, stamped from 55768a551a48, explain() identical) and 9b9e5d24ca9d (round-3 rulings).
